    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 2,168 -> 2811 (2026-08-25). The owner, handing over the Nathan Dean
    # cut: "we need to have more vocab ... expand our library of vocab of knowing
    # that this comes up."
    #
    # Same rule as every entry above it and it is the only rule that matters: the
    # VALUE is a physical thing a camera can point at. A finance or policy
    # interview's nouns are overwhelmingly abstractions, and the literal
    # illustration of an abstraction is a screen full of prices - which is the one
    # picture that must never go on camera. So "merger" is two people shaking
    # hands, "pension" is somebody on a park bench, "sanctions" is a container
    # port. No proper noun that would return an identifiable living person; a
    # named INSTITUTION resolves to its building, which is a place.
    #
    # 643 entries, checked against the live dict before splicing: zero collide.
    # 543 of them reuse a picture string already approved here, which is the
    # point - the library on disk is finite, and a new word that lands on a
    # picture already baked and already exposure-normalised costs one line and no
    # download. _lint_vocabulary() runs at import and refuses any value the
    # compliance filter would block outright.
    # --- markets, data and institutions ---
