# ---------------------------------------------------------- concrete first ----
# THE KEYS WHOSE PICTURE IS A METAPHOR, NOT THE THING ITSELF.
#
# The 2026-08-25 expansion did what the owner asked and gave abstract words a
# picture - "decide" a crossroads signpost, "doubt" a forked path, "accumulating"
# a snowball. That is right, and it introduced a ranking problem the moment it
# landed, because candidates() breaks a tie on LENGTH:
#
#     "They have been accumulating gold"   -> accumulating (12) beat gold (4)
#     "The Fed will decide in December"    -> decide (6) beat Fed (3)
#     "worried about the debt"             -> worried (7) beat debt (4)
#     "remembered the refinery shutdown"   -> remembered (10) beat refinery (8)
#
# Every one of those cuts to a metaphor while the speaker names a real subject
# standing right beside it. Measured over 2,689 sentences of this rig's own
# transcripts, an abstract key was the pick on 520 of them - 19.3% - and 168 of
# those had a concrete subject in the same group that should have won.
#
# So concreteness is a RANK TIER, above length: when a group holds both, the
# thing the sentence is ABOUT wins, and the metaphor is what you fall back on
# when there is nothing physical in the sentence at all. That is exactly when a
# metaphor earns its place.
#
# The list is the two abstraction batches' own keys plus every key sharing one of
# their pictures - provenance, not a guess about what "abstract" means.
